<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Exam problems on functions of several variables
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-functions-several-variables" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-functions-several-variables" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems find domains, level curves and level surfaces. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="chapter-functions-several-variables" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="pp-26">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the domain of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{y - 3x}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The square root is defined exactly when its argument is non-negative,
so the domain is
<md>
\{(x,y) : y - 3x \ge 0\} = \{(x,y) : y \ge 3x\},
</md>
the closed half-plane on and above the line <m>y = 3x</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-domain"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-domain">
<caption>
The domain of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{y-3x}</m> is the shaded closed
half-plane <m>y \ge 3x</m>, including the boundary line itself.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-domain">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-6.6,3.4,6.6)">
<polygon at="dm-region" points="((-2,-6),(-3.2,-6),(-3.2,6.4),(2.13,6.4))"
         fill="#56B4E9" fill-opacity="0.35" stroke="none"/>
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="dm-line" endpoints="((-2.13,-6.4),(2.13,6.4))"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="dm-line-lab" anchor="(2.05,5.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=3x</m>
</label>
<label at="dm-reg-lab" anchor="(-1.9,2.0)" alignment="w">
<m>y\ge 3x</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A steep blue line of slope three through the origin, y equals three x. Everything on and above and to the left of the line is shaded light blue; this shaded closed half plane is the domain.">
  <annotation ref="dm-line" text="The boundary line y equals three x, which belongs to the domain."/>
  <annotation ref="dm-region" text="The shaded closed half plane y greater than or equal to three x, the domain of the function."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-27">
<statement>
<p>
In <xref ref="fig-pp-bad-contours"/> are level curves of
<m>g(x,y) = 2</m> and <m>g(x,y) = 3</m> for some function
<m>g(x,y)</m>. What's wrong?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-bad-contours">
<caption>
Two curves labelled <m>g = 2</m> and <m>g = 3</m> that cross each
other.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-bad-contours">
<diagram dimensions="(300,190)" margins="12">
<definition>d1(t) = (t, 0.55*t + 0.15*sin(2*t))</definition>
<definition>d2(t) = (t, 2.2 - 0.55*t - 0.15*sin(2*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.3,-0.35,4.3,2.6)">
<parametric-curve at="bc-1" function="d1" domain="(0,4)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="bc-2" function="d2" domain="(0,4)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="bc-P" p="(2,1.1)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="bc-P-lab" anchor="(2.05,1.0)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-l1" anchor="(-0.25,0.05)" alignment="e">
<m>g=2</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-l2" anchor="(-0.25,2.15)" alignment="e">
<m>g=3</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-r1" anchor="(4.1,2.15)" alignment="w">
<m>g=2</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-r2" anchor="(4.1,0.05)" alignment="w">
<m>g=3</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two curves cross in the middle of the picture at a point P. Reading the labels, one curve is marked g equals two at its left end and g equals two at its right end after crossing, while the other is marked g equals three at both of its ends, so the two different level curves intersect.">
  <annotation ref="bc-P" text="The point P where the two level curves cross."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Two level curves belonging to <em>different</em> values can never
intersect. At the point <m>P</m> where the drawn curves cross, the
first curve says <m>g(P) = 2</m> while the second says
<m>g(P) = 3</m>. Since <m>g</m> is a function, it assigns exactly one
output to the point <m>P</m>, so <m>2 = 3</m> — which is impossible.
Hence no function <m>g</m> can have the level curves shown.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-6">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>g</m> be a function of 3 variables defined by
<md>
g(x,y,z) = \sqrt{4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2}.
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the equation for the level surface of <m>g</m> through the point
<m>\left(2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right)</m> and sketch this level surface.
Label any points where the surface crosses a coordinate axis AND label
the value of <m>g</m> on the surface.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the normal line to the level surface you found in
part A at the point <m>\left(2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Evaluating <m>g</m> at the given point,
<md>
g\left(2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right)
= \sqrt{4(4) + 11 + 9(1)}
= \sqrt{36} = 6,
</md>
so the level surface is <m>g(x,y,z) = 6</m>, i.e.
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt{4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2} = 6
\amp \;\Rightarrow\;
4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2 = 36</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{x^2}{9} + \frac{y^2}{36} + \frac{z^2}{4} = 1.</mrow>
</md>
This is an <term>ellipsoid</term> with semi-axes <m>3</m>, <m>6</m>,
and <m>2</m>. It crosses the axes at <m>(\pm 3, 0, 0)</m>,
<m>(0, \pm 6, 0)</m>, and <m>(0, 0, \pm 2)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw17-ellipsoid"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
A normal vector to the level surface at a point is
<m>\nabla g</m> there; since <m>g = \sqrt{F}</m> with
<m>F = 4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2</m>, the gradients <m>\nabla g</m> and
<m>\nabla F</m> are parallel, so we may use the simpler one:
<md>
\nabla F = \langle 8x, 2y, 18z\rangle,
\qquad
\nabla F\big|_{\left(2,\sqrt{11},1\right)}
= \left\langle 16, 2\sqrt{11}, 18\right\rangle
= 2\left\langle 8, \sqrt{11}, 9\right\rangle.
</md>
Hence the normal line through <m>\left(2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right)</m> is
<md>
\mathbf r(t)
= \left\langle 2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right\rangle
+ t\left\langle 8, \sqrt{11}, 9\right\rangle,
</md>
i.e. <m>x = 2 + 8t</m>, <m>y = \sqrt{11}\,(1 + t)</m>,
<m>z = 1 + 9t</m>, <m>t \in \R</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw17-ellipsoid">
<caption>
The level surface <m>g = 6</m>, i.e. the ellipsoid
<m>\frac{x^2}{9} + \frac{y^2}{36} + \frac{z^2}{4} = 1</m>, with its
axis intercepts.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
An ellipsoid centered at the origin, elongated along the y axis. It
meets the x axis at plus and minus three, the y axis at plus and minus
six, and the z axis at plus and minus two. Three dashed ellipses show
its cross sections in the coordinate planes.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-1.9) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-3.6,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (1.15,0.82) -- (-1.5,-1.07) node[below left] {$x$};
  % the ellipsoid outline: semi-axis 3 along y (drawn), 1 along z (drawn)
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 1cm);
  % cross-section ellipses
  \draw[mblue, dashed] (0,0) ellipse (0.55cm and 1cm);
  \draw[mblue, dashed] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 0.28cm);
  % intercepts
  \fill (3,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=1pt] {$(0,6,0)$};
  \fill (-3,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$(0,-6,0)$};
  \fill (0,1) circle (1.6pt) node[above right=1pt] {$(0,0,2)$};
  \fill (0,-1) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=1pt] {$(0,0,-2)$};
  \fill (0.55,0.39) circle (1.6pt) node[above right=0pt] {$(3,0,0)$};
  \fill (-0.55,-0.39) circle (1.6pt);
  % label the level value
  \node[mblue] at (2.1,1.35) {$g=6$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-25">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the level surface <m>f(x,y,z) = z^2 - x^2 - y^2</m> for
<m>f(x,y,z) = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The level surface is <m>z^2 - x^2 - y^2 = 4</m>, i.e.
<md>
\frac{z^2}{4} - \frac{x^2}{4} - \frac{y^2}{4} = 1.
</md>
Rewriting as <m>z^2 = 4 + x^2 + y^2</m> shows that
<m>z^2 \ge 4</m>, so <m>z \ge 2</m> or <m>z \le -2</m>: no part of the
surface lies between the two horizontal planes <m>z = \pm 2</m>. The
traces are
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>z = c</m> with <m>|c| \gt 2</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 = c^2 - 4</m>,
circles;
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x = c</m>: <m>z^2 - y^2 = 4 + c^2</m>, hyperbolas;
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = c</m>: <m>z^2 - x^2 = 4 + c^2</m>, hyperbolas.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
Hence the level surface is a <term>hyperboloid of two sheets</term>,
symmetric about the <m>z</m>-axis, with vertices <m>(0,0,\pm 2)</m>.
See <xref ref="fig-pp-level-surface"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-level-surface">
<caption>
The level surface <m>z^2 - x^2 - y^2 = 4</m>: a hyperboloid of two
sheets about the <m>z</m>-axis with vertices <m>(0,0,\pm 2)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<description>
<p>
Two bowl shaped sheets in a three dimensional coordinate system, one
opening upward with its lowest point at zero comma zero comma two, and
its mirror image opening downward with highest point at zero comma
zero comma negative two. Each sheet is capped by an ellipse.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-3.7) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.9,0.65) -- (-1.25,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
  % upper sheet: x = +-0.85*sqrt(z^2-4) for z from 2 to 3.4
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, \x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({-0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, \x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,3.4) ellipse (2.25cm and 0.33cm);
  % lower sheet
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, -\x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({-0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, -\x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,-3.4) ellipse (2.25cm and 0.33cm);
  % vertices
  \fill (0,2) circle (1.6pt) node[right=3pt] {$(0,0,2)$};
  \fill (0,-2) circle (1.6pt) node[right=3pt] {$(0,0,-2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<md>
h(x,y,z) = z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} + 1 .
</md>
Sketch the level surface of <m>h</m> that passes through the point
<m>(3,4,-5)</m>. Clearly label the value of <m>h</m> on that surface,
and label the coordinate axes.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The level surfaces of <m>h</m> are the surfaces <m>h(x,y,z) = c</m>.
To find which one passes through <m>(3,4,-5)</m> we simply evaluate:
<md>
<mrow>h(3,4,-5) \amp = -5 + \sqrt{3^2+4^2} + 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -5 + \sqrt{25} + 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -5 + 5 + 1 = 1 .</mrow>
</md>
So the level surface we want is <m>h(x,y,z) = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now we simplify the equation of that level surface:
<md>
<mrow>z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} + 1 \amp = 1</mrow>
<mrow>z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>z \amp = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
This is a cone. Indeed, squaring gives <m>z^2 = x^2+y^2</m>, which is
the full double cone; but the equation <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>
forces <m>z \le 0</m>, so we keep only the <em>lower</em> nappe. The
vertex is at the origin, and the horizontal trace at height
<m>z = -k</m> (with <m>k \gt 0</m>) is the circle
<m>x^2+y^2 = k^2</m> of radius <m>k</m>. In other words the cone opens
downwards, and the slope of every straight line drawn on it from the
vertex is <m>-1</m>. The point <m>(3,4,-5)</m> lies on it, since
<m>-\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = -5</m>. See <xref ref="fig-q1-cone"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q1-cone">
<caption>
The level surface <m>h(x,y,z) = 1</m>, that is the downward cone
<m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>. It passes through <m>(3,4,-5)</m>,
which lies on the circle <m>x^2+y^2=25</m> in the plane <m>z=-5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A cone with its vertex at the origin opening downwards around the
negative z axis. The horizontal circle of radius five is drawn on it in
the plane z equals negative five, and the point three, four, negative
five is marked on that circle.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{18},
  xmin=-6.5, xmax=6.5, ymin=-6.5, ymax=6.5, zmin=-6.5, zmax=2.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-5,0,5}, ytick={-5,0,5}, ztick={-5,0},
  clip=false,
]
% the cone z = -sqrt(x^2+y^2), drawn in polar form
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=mblue, draw=mblue,
          fill opacity=0.30, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:5.6, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{-x});
% the trace circle at z = -5
\addplot3[very thick, mverm, domain=0:360, samples=60]
  ({5*cos(x)},{5*sin(x)},{-5});
% the marked point (3,4,-5)
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2pt, mred] coordinates {(3,4,-5)};
\node[mred, anchor=west, fill=white, fill opacity=0.75, text opacity=1,
      inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:3,4,-5) {$(3,4,-5)$};
\node[mverm, anchor=north] at (axis cs:0,-5.6,-5.6)
  {$x^2+y^2=25$};
\node[mblue, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:0.72,0.86)
  {$h=1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q2-1">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch three level curves of
<md>
h(x,y) = \frac{y^2}{x} ,
</md>
including the one that passes through the point <m>(-1,2)</m>. Clearly
label the value of <m>h</m> on each curve.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First we find the level through <m>(-1,2)</m>:
<md>
h(-1,2) = \frac{2^2}{-1} = -4 .
</md>
So one of the three curves must be <m>h(x,y) = -4</m>.
</p>

<p>
In general the level curve <m>h(x,y) = c</m> is
<md>
\frac{y^2}{x} = c \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad y^2 = c\,x
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x = \frac{y^2}{c}
\qquad (c \ne 0) .
</md>
Each of these is a <em>parabola opening along the</em> <m>x</m><em>-axis</em>,
with vertex at the origin. If <m>c \gt 0</m> it opens to the right, and
if <m>c \lt 0</m> it opens to the left. Note that the domain of
<m>h</m> excludes <m>x = 0</m>, so the vertex itself, the origin, must
be removed from every level curve.
</p>

<p>
Let us take the three levels <m>c = -4</m>, <m>c = 1</m> and
<m>c = 4</m>:
<md>
<mrow>h \amp = -4: \amp\qquad x \amp = -\frac{y^2}{4}
  \amp\amp \text{(opens left)},</mrow>
<mrow>h \amp = 1: \amp\qquad x \amp = y^2
  \amp\amp \text{(opens right, narrow)},</mrow>
<mrow>h \amp = 4: \amp\qquad x \amp = \frac{y^2}{4}
  \amp\amp \text{(opens right, wide)}.</mrow>
</md>
The larger <m>|c|</m> is, the wider the parabola. These are drawn in
<xref ref="fig-q2-levels"/>.
</p>

<p>
One more remark: the level <m>c = 0</m> is special. There
<m>y^2 = 0</m>, so the level curve <m>h = 0</m> is not a parabola at
all but the <m>x</m>-axis with the origin removed.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q2-levels">
<caption>
Three level curves of <m>h(x,y) = y^2/x</m>. The curve through
<m>(-1,2)</m> is the level <m>h = -4</m>, which opens to the left. The
origin is excluded from every level curve, since <m>h</m> is undefined
when <m>x = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
Three parabolas with vertices at the origin and axes along the x axis.
One opens to the left and is labelled h equals negative four; two open
to the right and are labelled h equals one and h equals four. The point
negative one comma two is marked on the leftward parabola.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-q2-levels">
<diagram dimensions="(430,380)" margins="30">
<definition>cm4(t) = (-t^2/4, t)</definition>
<definition>c1(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t^2/4, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-3.4,4.6,3.9)">
<grid-axes at="q2l-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-m4" function="cm4" domain="(-3.2,3.2)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-1" function="c1" domain="(-2.0,2.0)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-4" function="c4" domain="(-3.2,3.2)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="q2l-P" p="(-1,2)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="q2l-Plab" anchor="(-1.20,2.18)" alignment="nw" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>(-1,2)</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-m4lab" anchor="(-2.9,3.45)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>h=-4</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-1lab" anchor="(4.45,1.60)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>h=1</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-4lab" anchor="(3.35,3.45)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>h=4</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three parabolas sharing the origin as vertex and the x axis as axis of symmetry. An orange one opens to the left, labelled h equals negative four. A blue narrow one and a green wide one open to the right, labelled h equals one and h equals four. A dark red dot marks the point negative one comma two on the orange curve.">
  <annotation ref="q2l-m4" text="The level curve h equals negative four, the parabola x equals minus y squared over four, opening to the left."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-1" text="The level curve h equals one, the parabola x equals y squared, opening to the right."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-4" text="The level curve h equals four, the parabola x equals y squared over four, opening to the right and wider than the previous one."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-P" text="The point negative one comma two, which lies on the level curve h equals negative four."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
